# Flutter MVC Architecture

Use MVC for all Flutter features in this project. Keep Model, View, and Controller responsibilities separate.

## Model
- Data models, entities, request/response types, serialization only
- Must not contain Flutter UI code
- Must not depend on Widgets or `BuildContext`

## View
- Flutter Widgets: layout, styling, rendering, user interaction
- No business logic and no direct API/network calls
- Communicate with Controllers for actions and state changes

## Controller
- Coordinates Views and Models
- Handles user actions and presentation/UI-related state
- Calls services/repositories for data
- Keeps business logic out of Widgets

## Hard rules
- Always follow MVC when creating or changing a feature
- Do not put business logic in Widgets or UI logic in Models
- Do not make network/API calls from Views
- Follow the existing project structure; do not invent new folder patterns
- Do not introduce Clean Architecture, MVVM, Riverpod architecture, or another pattern unless explicitly requested

## New features
1. Define Model responsibilities
2. Define Controller responsibilities
3. Build the View with reusable Widgets
4. Connect the View to the Controller
5. Keep data/networking outside the View

```dart
// ❌ BAD — logic / API in the View
class AnimalListPage extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    // fetchAnimals() called from build / onPressed in the widget
    return ListView(...);
  }
}

// ✅ GOOD — View delegates to Controller
class AnimalListPage extends StatelessWidget {
  const AnimalListPage({required this.controller});
  final AnimalListController controller;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return ListView(
      children: [
        for (final animal in controller.animals) Text(animal.name),
      ],
    );
  }
}
```
